<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.jrobertgardzinski</groupId>
    <artifactId>transactional-outbox</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <!--
      The transactional outbox as a library (like the voting and password libs): "an event whose
      transaction committed WILL eventually reach the broker", extracted from the two working
      implementations the portal grew independently — microservice-memes' hardened MEME_DELETED
      outbox and microservice-comments' simpler after-commit publication. The audit of 2026-07-26
      named the duplication as its motif no. 2: the participant's role was copied four times and
      every copy carried a different guarantee.

      FRAMEWORK-FREE ON PURPOSE. Consumers sit on Spring Boot, Micronaut and Quarkus, so the core
      may not drag any of them in — and it may not depend on a Kafka client either: sending is an
      INTERFACE (Dispatch) the service implements with whatever producer it already has. The only
      runtime dependencies are the JDK's own java.sql and slf4j-api, the logging facade every one
      of those frameworks already binds.

      The Spring wiring (current-transaction append, @Scheduled republisher, publish-after-commit)
      lives in the sibling module infrastructure-spring-outbox — the same split as
      adjustable-clock + infrastructure-micronaut-clock.
    -->
    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <!-- the one runtime dependency: a logging FACADE, no binding. The library's warnings
                 (payload canary, retention cap reached, unconfirmed sends) are what an operator
                 debugs a stuck outbox from, so they must reach the host's log configuration. -->
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
            <version>2.0.16</version>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>6.1.2</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.qameta.allure</groupId>
            <artifactId>allure-junit5</artifactId>
            <version>2.29.0</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- H2 in PostgreSQL mode: the library's tests run the REAL SQL the library ships,
                 against the same dialect the portal's services run their JDBC adapters against.
                 No service, no broker, no container needed to prove the durability promise. -->
            <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
            <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
            <version>2.3.232</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- a binding for the tests only, so a failing run shows the library's own warnings -->
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-simple</artifactId>
            <version>2.0.16</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.13.0</version>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.2</version>
                <configuration>
                    <systemPropertyVariables>
                        <!-- under target/, so mvn clean removes them and reports never accumulate stale runs -->
                        <allure.results.directory>${project.build.directory}/allure-results</allure.results.directory>
                    </systemPropertyVariables>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <!-- allure reports everywhere -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>io.qameta.allure</groupId>
                <artifactId>allure-maven</artifactId>
                <version>2.17.0</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
